From: Yury Klushin <digger@live.ru>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 16:00:34 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Add RT-11 SAV output support for pdp11-uknc, with a dedicated
 rt11 OS

Adds a self-contained pipeline for producing RT-11 ".sav" executables
for UKNC (pdp11-uknc-*) directly from this toolchain, replacing the
project's previous external bin2load/lda2sav conversion step:

- bfd/sav-pdp11.c: a new write-only BFD target that emits RT-11 SAV
  executables (512-byte header with start address, initial SP, and
  high-limit fields, plus a block-usage bitmap) from a flat 64K PDP-11
  memory image built up via the normal BFD section-content path.

- config.sub: recognizes "uknc" as a vendor alias for pdp11-uknc-aout,
  and "rt11*" as a valid OS component for the pdp11 target triple
  (pdp11-uknc-rt11), alongside the existing aout-based triple.
  gas/configure.tgt maps the rt11 OS to the aout object format, same
  as plain aout targets.

- ld/emulparams/pdp11rt11.sh + ld/scripttempl/pdp11rt11.sc: a built-in
  default linker script/emulation (matching this project's previous
  external pdp11-aout.ld) so pdp11-uknc-aout's `ld` no longer requires
  an explicit -T. The script includes a .bss output section bracketed
  by __bss_start before and _end/__end after (matching vanilla
  pdp11.sc's exact pattern) -- without it bss fell through as an
  orphan section placed *after* PROVIDE(end = .), so "end" never
  actually marked the top of statically-allocated memory the way it
  does for the generic pdp11.sc script, which breaks anything relying
  on _end to find the start of free memory (e.g. a newlib-style _sbrk
  heap, or zeroing .bss at startup before it's known where .bss
  actually ends).

- bfd/config.bfd / bfd/targets.c: sav-pdp11 is only compiled visible
  (-DBFD_SUPPORTS_SAV_PDP11, gated via #ifdef in targets.c) for the
  rt11 OS; pdp11-uknc-aout stays byte-for-byte identical to vanilla
  binutils, with no sav-pdp11 target and no default script override.

- ld/configure.tgt: pdp11-*-rt11* defaults to a new pdp11rt11sav
  emulation (falling back to pdp11rt11, then pdp11).  Its
  OUTPUT_FORMAT is deliberately still "a.out-pdp11", not "sav-pdp11":
  linking straight to sav-pdp11 would go through BFD's generic
  final-link path (sav-pdp11 has no relocation-resolution code of its
  own), which does not correctly reproduce a.out-pdp11's own
  specialized one (pdp11_aout_link_input_section in bfd/pdp11.c) --
  confirmed wrong on something as simple as a string-constant address
  reference.  Instead, pdp11rt11sav links a real, correct a.out-pdp11
  file first, then converts it to RT-11 SAV format in place once that
  file is fully written to disk -- a relocation-free repack, the same
  conversion `objcopy -O sav-pdp11` already does correctly.  That
  conversion runs from a new optional ld emulation hook,
  after_close_output (ld/ldemul.h, ld/ldemul.c,
  ld/emultempl/emulation.em, one call site in ld/ldmain.c added right
  after ldwrite()'s bfd_close()); it defaults to NULL/no-op for every
  other emulation.  ld/emultempl/pdp11rt11sav.em implements it for
  pdp11rt11sav by reopening the just-written a.out-pdp11 file, copying
  its sections into a sav-pdp11 bfd, and swapping it in for the
  original output file.

- The RT-11 SAV header's initial-SP field is set to the top of user
  memory (the address immediately below the resident RT-11 monitor and
  the fixed Unibus I/O page), not the program's entry point -- RT-11
  copies this value into low memory before transferring control, so a
  program's startup code can pick it up via `mov @$042,sp` instead of
  hardcoding a stack address of its own.

Verified: a fresh pdp11-uknc-aout build exposes only the pdp11
emulation and no sav-pdp11 target (byte-identical to vanilla
binutils); a fresh pdp11-uknc-rt11 build's single-command
`ld -m pdp11rt11sav` produces output byte-identical to the two-step
`ld -m pdp11rt11 && objcopy -O sav-pdp11` pipeline on a test program
with a string-constant address relocation. The .bss fix was verified
separately with a real rebuild of ld and a SIMH test program with a
global .bss variable: __bss_start/_end now correctly bracket .bss.

Includes what was a separate fix on top of this one, now part of it:

Fix pdp11-uknc RT-11 SAV writer setting SP below the program's own size

sav_pdp11_write_object_contents() always wrote the hardcoded
SAV_STACK_ADDRESS (16896) as the SAV header's initial-SP word,
regardless of how large the linked program actually was. RT-11 copies
that word into low memory at address 042 before transferring control,
and crt0 reads it back as a provisional SP -- used as-is until crt0's
own `.SETTOP` EMT replaces it with the monitor's real answer a few
instructions later.

A program whose own code/data (including .bss) extends past 16896
bytes -- easy once printf/rand pull in enough of libc/libgcc -- got a
provisional SP that pointed *inside* its own already-loaded text. An
EMT's trap entry pushes the return PC/PS onto whatever SP already
holds, so that very first `.SETTOP` EMT silently corrupted the
program's own code before a single instruction of its own body ran.

Found via bisection on a real crash (`?MON-F-Trap to 10`) in a ~20KB
UKNC C program: the corrupted byte sat exactly 20 bytes below the
hardcoded 16896 boundary, inside __muldi3 (pulled in from libgcc),
matching an EMT trap-frame push against a too-low provisional SP.
Reproduced identically on SIMH (unrelated, non-UKNC PDP-11 emulator),
which ruled out any UKNC-hardware-specific cause and pointed at the
SAV file itself.

Fix: compute the highest address used by any SEC_ALLOC section
(including .bss, which carries no file content but is still live
memory crt0 must not stack on top of) and write
max(SAV_STACK_ADDRESS, that highest address) as the SP word instead.
Small programs that already fit under the default are unaffected.

Verified: rebuilt binutils, relinked a previously-crashing ~20KB
program, confirmed the SP word now reflects the real top of memory,
and ran the program to completion (many rounds of interactive I/O)
without a single trap, both on SIMH and on the real UKNC emulator core
(ukncbtl's emubase). All other example programs in the toolchain
rebuild unchanged.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
